\ The words the compiler used to know. Every program sees these without an
\ `import:`, exactly as it sees `bool`; nothing else about them is special.
\
\ The compiler is left with three machine-level primitives: `branch`, which
\ jumps on a 32-bit flag and takes two quotations; `tag`, which reads a
\ payload-free enum's discriminant as that flag; and the six comparison
\ primitives `ueq`/`ult`/`ugt`/`ulte`/`ugte`/`une`, which produce it. Everything
\ typed -- `bool`, `if`, `unless`, `eq` -- is written here, in Sooth.

\ The comparisons. Each is `'T: Copy Ord`-polymorphic, so one definition
\ covers the whole numeric tower rather than narrowing the language to `i64`,
\ and each is `inline`, so it is spliced at its call site: without that every
\ comparison in every program would become a real call with a frame. The
\ branch-and-construct body is also what makes an invalid `bool` impossible to
\ build -- there is deliberately no operation turning a machine word back into
\ an enum, only these two named variants.
: eq  inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- bool ) ueq  [ true ] [ false ] branch ;
: lt  inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- bool ) ult  [ true ] [ false ] branch ;
: gt  inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- bool ) ugt  [ true ] [ false ] branch ;
: lte inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- bool ) ulte [ true ] [ false ] branch ;
: gte inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- bool ) ugte [ true ] [ false ] branch ;
: ne  inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- bool ) une  [ true ] [ false ] branch ;

\ `if` reads its `bool`'s discriminant and branches on it. Both quotations are
\ `~` (inline-only) and share the input row `..a` and the output row `..b`, so
\ a branch may change the stack shape as long as both agree; the shape change
\ is splice-local and reconciled by the branch's own join.
\
\ The locals are spelled out rather than abbreviated because this body is
\ checked against the whole program's word environment: a local may not shadow
\ a callable name, so a one-letter local here would collide with any program
\ that happens to define a word `t`. They are also prefixed `if--`/`unless--`
\ rather than left as plain `cond`/`then-arm`/`else-arm`: `cond` is DESIGN.md's
\ documented future multi-way branch word, so a bare `cond` local here would
\ collide with the very word this file is meant to grow. (The underlying gap
\ -- prelude-word locals have no hygiene against user or future stdlib names
\ at all -- is not fixed by this; it is a separate slice.)
\
\ Its tail term is `branch`, whose tail-called parameters are both quotations,
\ so a caller's `... ~[ base ] ~[ ... rec ] if` self-tails through it and lowers
\ to a loop back-edge rather than recursion.
: if inline ( ..a bool ~[ ..a -- ..b ] ~[ ..a -- ..b ] -- ..b )
  | if--else-arm | | if--then-arm | | if--cond |
  if--cond tag if--then-arm if--else-arm branch ;

\ `unless` is `if` with the arms swapped: the first quotation runs when the
\ condition is false.
: unless inline ( ..a bool ~[ ..a -- ..b ] ~[ ..a -- ..b ] -- ..b )
  | unless--else-arm | | unless--then-arm | | unless--cond |
  unless--cond tag unless--else-arm unless--then-arm branch ;
